                    "id": "generating-functions",
                    "label": "Generating Functions & Theorems",
                    "content": "## Generating Functions & Key Theorems\n\n### Moment Generating Function (MGF)\n\n\\[\nM_X(t) = E(e^{tX}) = \\sum_{r=0}^\\infty \\frac{\\mu'_r\\, t^r}{r!}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mu'_r = \\left.\\frac{d^r}{dt^r} M_X(t)\\right|_{t=0}\n\\]\n\nMGF **uniquely determines** the distribution (when it exists in a neighbourhood of 0).\n\nIf \\(X \\perp Y\\): \\(M_{X+Y}(t) = M_X(t)\\cdot M_Y(t)\\)\n\n---\n\n### Cumulant Generating Function\n\n\\[\nK_X(t) = \\ln M_X(t) = \\sum_{r=1}^\\infty \\frac{\\kappa_r\\, t^r}{r!}\n\\]\n\n**Cumulant–Moment relations:**\n\\[\n\\kappa_1 = \\mu'_1 = \\mu, \\quad \\kappa_2 = \\mu_2 = \\sigma^2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\kappa_3 = \\mu_3, \\quad \\kappa_4 = \\mu_4 - 3\\mu_2^2\n\\]\n\nCumulants are **additive** for independent RVs: \\(\\kappa_r(X+Y) = \\kappa_r(X) + \\kappa_r(Y)\\)\n\n---\n\n### Probability Generating Function (PGF)\n\nFor **non-negative integer** valued \\(X\\):\n\\[\nG_X(s) = E(s^X) = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty P(X=k)\\, s^k\n\\]\n\\[\nP(X=k) = \\frac{1}{k!} G_X^{(k)}(0), \\quad E(X) = G'_X(1), \\quad E[X(X-1)] = G''_X(1)\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Characteristic Function (CF)\n\n\\[\n\\phi_X(t) = E(e^{itX}) = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{itx} f(x)\\,dx\n\\]\n\n**Properties:**\n- \\(\\phi_X(0) = 1\\), \\(|\\phi_X(t)| \\le 1\\) for all \\(t\\)\n- CF always exists (unlike MGF)\n- If \\(X \\perp Y\\): \\(\\phi_{X+Y}(t) = \\phi_X(t)\\cdot\\phi_Y(t)\\)\n- \\(\\mu'_r = \\dfrac{1}{i^r}\\phi_X^{(r)}(0)\\)\n\n**Inversion Theorem:** The CF uniquely determines the distribution:\n\\[\nf(x) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-itx}\\phi_X(t)\\,dt\n\\]\n\n**Uniqueness Theorem:** If \\(\\phi_X(t) = \\phi_Y(t)\\) for all \\(t\\), then \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) have the same distribution.\n\n**Continuity Theorem (Lévy):** \\(F_n \\xrightarrow{d} F\\) if and only if \\(\\phi_n(t) \\to \\phi(t)\\) pointwise for all \\(t\\). This is the key tool for proving CLT.\n\n---\n\n### CFs of Key Distributions\n\n| Distribution | CF \\(\\phi_X(t)\\) |\n|---|---|\n| \\(N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\\) | \\(e^{i\\mu t - \\sigma^2 t^2/2}\\) |\n| Cauchy\\((\\mu,1)\\) | \\(e^{i\\mu t - |t|}\\) |\n| Poisson\\((\\lambda)\\) | \\(e^{\\lambda(e^{it}-1)}\\) |\n| Bernoulli\\((p)\\) | \\(q + pe^{it}\\) |\n| \\(\\text{Gamma}(\\alpha,\\lambda)\\) | \\((1 - it/\\lambda)^{-\\alpha}\\) |\n\n> **ISS tip:** The CF exists for ALL distributions; the MGF may not (e.g. Cauchy). The Continuity Theorem is the standard proof tool for CLT — expect a question on this. Cauchy's CF has no moments; its MGF does not exist."
