                    "id": "convergence-laws",
                    "label": "Convergence, Inequalities & Limit Laws",
                    "content": "## Convergence of Random Variables, Inequalities & Limit Laws\n\n### Four Modes of Convergence\n\nLet \\(X_n, X\\) be random variables on the same probability space.\n\n**1. In Distribution (Weak, \\(\\xrightarrow{d}\\)):**\n\\[\nX_n \\xrightarrow{d} X \\iff F_n(x) \\to F(x) \\text{ at every continuity point of } F\n\\]\nWeakest mode. Only about CDFs, not the RVs themselves.\n\n**2. In Probability (\\(\\xrightarrow{p}\\)):**\n\\[\nX_n \\xrightarrow{p} X \\iff P(|X_n - X| > \\varepsilon) \\to 0 \\text{ for every } \\varepsilon > 0\n\\]\n\n**3. Almost Surely (with probability 1, \\(\\xrightarrow{a.s.}\\)):**\n\\[\nX_n \\xrightarrow{a.s.} X \\iff P\\!\\left(\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} X_n = X\\right) = 1\n\\]\n\n**4. In Mean Square (\\(L^2\\), \\(\\xrightarrow{m.s.}\\)):**\n\\[\nX_n \\xrightarrow{m.s.} X \\iff E(X_n - X)^2 \\to 0\n\\]\n\n### Hierarchy of Convergence\n\n\\[\na.s. \\Rightarrow p \\Rightarrow d\n\\]\n\\[\nm.s. \\Rightarrow p \\Rightarrow d\n\\]\n\nNeither a.s. nor m.s. implies the other in general.\n\n---\n\n### Inequalities\n\n**Markov's Inequality:** For \\(X \\ge 0\\), \\(a > 0\\):\n\\[\nP(X \\ge a) \\le \\frac{E(X)}{a}\n\\]\n\n**Chebyshev's (Tchebycheff's) Inequality:**\n\\[\nP(|X - \\mu| \\ge k\\sigma) \\le \\frac{1}{k^2} \\quad \\text{or equivalently} \\quad P(|X-\\mu| \\ge \\varepsilon) \\le \\frac{\\sigma^2}{\\varepsilon^2}\n\\]\n\n**Kolmogorov's Inequality:** For independent \\(X_1,\\ldots,X_n\\) with \\(E(X_i)=0\\), \\(\\text{Var}(X_i)<\\infty\\):\n\\[\nP\\!\\left(\\max_{1\\le k\\le n} |S_k| \\ge \\varepsilon\\right) \\le \\frac{\\text{Var}(S_n)}{\\varepsilon^2}, \\quad S_k = X_1+\\cdots+X_k\n\\]\nThis strengthens Chebyshev; fundamental in SLLN proof.\n\n---\n\n### Zero-One Laws\n\n**Borel–Cantelli Lemma:**\n- **Part 1:** If \\(\\sum P(A_n) < \\infty\\), then \\(P(A_n \\text{ i.o.}) = 0\\)\n- **Part 2:** If \\(A_n\\) independent and \\(\\sum P(A_n) = \\infty\\), then \\(P(A_n \\text{ i.o.}) = 1\\)\n\n**Borel 0-1 Law:** For any tail event \\(A\\) of independent RVs: \\(P(A) = 0\\) or \\(P(A) = 1\\)\n\n**Kolmogorov 0-1 Law:** Every tail event of independent random variables has probability 0 or 1. Examples of tail events: \\(\\{\\sum X_n \\text{ converges}\\}\\), \\(\\{\\limsup S_n/n = c\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Laws of Large Numbers\n\n**Weak LLN (WLLN — Khintchine):** For i.i.d. \\(X_i\\) with \\(E(X_i) = \\mu\\):\n\\[\n\\bar{X}_n = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\xrightarrow{p} \\mu\n\\]\n\n**Strong LLN (SLLN — Kolmogorov):** Under the same conditions:\n\\[\n\\bar{X}_n \\xrightarrow{a.s.} \\mu\n\\]\n\nSLLN requires only finite mean (not finite variance). SLLN ⟹ WLLN.\n\n---\n\n### Central Limit Theorem\n\n**Lindeberg–Lévy CLT** (i.i.d., finite variance):\n\nFor i.i.d. \\(X_i\\) with \\(E(X_i) = \\mu\\), \\(\\text{Var}(X_i) = \\sigma^2 < \\infty\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\bar{X}_n - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} = \\frac{S_n - n\\mu}{\\sigma\\sqrt{n}} \\xrightarrow{d} N(0,1)\n\\]\n\n**Liapounov CLT** (independent, not necessarily identical): If \\(\\exists\\, \\delta > 0\\) such that \\(\\dfrac{\\sum E|X_i - \\mu_i|^{2+\\delta}}{\\left(\\sum \\sigma_i^2\\right)^{1+\\delta/2}} \\to 0\\), then CLT holds.\n\n**Normal Approximation to Binomial** (de Moivre–Laplace): \\(X \\sim B(n,p)\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{X - np}{\\sqrt{npq}} \\xrightarrow{d} N(0,1)\n\\]\nUse continuity correction: \\(P(X \\le k) \\approx \\Phi\\!\\left(\\dfrac{k + 0.5 - np}{\\sqrt{npq}}\\right)\\)\n\n> **ISS tip:** Know the hierarchy a.s. ⟹ p ⟹ d. Chebyshev's formula in both forms is frequently tested. Borel–Cantelli Part 1 (convergent series → probability 0) and Part 2 (divergent + independent → probability 1) are direct MCQ targets. CLT: the condition is finite variance, not finite mean alone."
