                {
                    "id": "num-finite-diff",
                    "label": "Finite Differences & Operators",
                    "content": "## Finite Differences & Operators\n\n### The Three Core Operators\n\n| Operator | Symbol | Definition |\n|---|---|---|\n| Forward Difference | \\(\\Delta\\) | \\(\\Delta f(x) = f(x+h) - f(x)\\) |\n| Backward Difference | \\(\\nabla\\) | \\(\\nabla f(x) = f(x) - f(x-h)\\) |\n| Shift | \\(E\\) | \\(E\\,f(x) = f(x+h)\\) |\n| Central Difference | \\(\\delta\\) | \\(\\delta f(x) = f(x+h/2) - f(x-h/2)\\) |\n\n### Key Operator Relations\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = E - 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad E = 1 + \\Delta\n\\]\n\\[\n\\nabla = 1 - E^{-1} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad E^{-1} = 1 - \\nabla\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Delta \\equiv E - 1, \\quad \\nabla \\equiv 1 - E^{-1}, \\quad \\delta \\equiv E^{1/2} - E^{-1/2}\n\\]\n\n**Averaging operator** \\(\\mu\\):\n\\[\n\\mu\\,f(x) = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\bigl[f(x+h/2) + f(x-h/2)\\bigr]\n\\]\n\n### Higher-Order Forward Differences\n\n\\[\n\\Delta^n f(x) = \\sum_{r=0}^{n} (-1)^{n-r}\\binom{n}{r} f(x+rh)\n\\]\n\n**Differences of zero** (useful for polynomial recognition):\n\\[\n\\Delta^n(0) = \\sum_{r=0}^{n}(-1)^{n-r}\\binom{n}{r}r^n = n!\n\\]\nFor a polynomial of degree \\(n\\): \\(\\Delta^n f\\) = constant, \\(\\Delta^{n+1} f = 0\\).\n\n### Factorial Notation\n\n\\[\nx^{(n)} = x(x-1)(x-2)\\cdots(x-n+1) \\quad \\text{(falling factorial)}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Delta\\, x^{(n)} = n\\, x^{(n-1)}\n\\]\nAny polynomial can be written in factorial form — this simplifies differencing enormously.\n\n### Separation of Symbols\n\nSince \\(E = e^{hD}\\) (where \\(D = d/dx\\)):\n\\[\n\\Delta = e^{hD} - 1, \\qquad hD = \\ln(1+\\Delta)\n\\]\nUseful for converting between difference and differential operators.\n\n### Sub-division of Intervals\n\nIf the interval is subdivided from \\(h\\) to \\(h/m\\), use \\(E^{1/m}\\) and the relation:\n\\[\n\\Delta_h = \\Delta_{h/m}^m\n\\]\n\n> **ISS tip:** Questions frequently test: identifying the degree of a polynomial from its difference table, operator algebra (simplify \\(\\Delta^2 E\\), etc.), and differences of zero."
                },
                {
                    "id": "num-interpolation-equal",
                    "label": "Interpolation — Equal Intervals",
                    "content": "## Interpolation for Equal Intervals\n\nAll formulae below use \\( p = (x - x_0)/h \\) as the substitution variable.\n\n---\n\n### Newton–Gregory Forward Interpolation\n\n**Use:** \\(x\\) near the **beginning** of the table.\n\n\\[\nf(x) = f_0 + p\\,\\Delta f_0 + \\frac{p(p-1)}{2!}\\Delta^2 f_0 + \\frac{p(p-1)(p-2)}{3!}\\Delta^3 f_0 + \\cdots\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p = \\dfrac{x - x_0}{h}\\), \\(\\;0 < p < 1\\) for interpolation.\n\n**Error term:**\n\\[\nR_n = \\frac{p(p-1)\\cdots(p-n)}{(n+1)!}\\,h^{n+1}\\,f^{(n+1)}(\\xi)\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Newton–Gregory Backward Interpolation\n\n**Use:** \\(x\\) near the **end** of the table.\n\nLet \\( p = (x - x_n)/h \\) (here \\(p\\) is negative for backward interpolation from the last entry).\n\n\\[\nf(x) = f_n + p\\,\\nabla f_n + \\frac{p(p+1)}{2!}\\nabla^2 f_n + \\frac{p(p+1)(p+2)}{3!}\\nabla^3 f_n + \\cdots\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Central Difference Formulae (middle of table)\n\n**Gauss Forward Formula** (\\(0 < p < 1\\)):\n\\[\nf(x) = f_0 + p\\,\\delta f_{1/2} + \\frac{p(p-1)}{2!}\\delta^2 f_0 + \\frac{(p+1)p(p-1)}{3!}\\delta^3 f_{1/2} + \\cdots\n\\]\n\n**Gauss Backward Formula** (\\(-1 < p < 0\\)):\n\\[\nf(x) = f_0 + p\\,\\delta f_{-1/2} + \\frac{p(p+1)}{2!}\\delta^2 f_0 + \\frac{(p+1)p(p-1)}{3!}\\delta^3 f_{-1/2} + \\cdots\n\\]\n\n**Stirling's Formula** (average of Gauss forward & backward, best near centre):\n\\[\nf(x) = f_0 + p\\,\\mu\\delta f_0 + \\frac{p^2}{2!}\\delta^2 f_0 + \\frac{p(p^2-1)}{3!}\\mu\\delta^3 f_0 + \\frac{p^2(p^2-1)}{4!}\\delta^4 f_0 + \\cdots\n\\]\n\n**Bessel's Formula** (best when \\(p \\approx 1/2\\)):\n\\[\nf(x) = \\tfrac{1}{2}(f_0+f_1) + \\bigl(p-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\bigr)\\delta f_{1/2} + \\frac{p(p-1)}{2!}\\cdot\\tfrac{1}{2}(\\delta^2 f_0+\\delta^2 f_1) + \\cdots\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Quick Selection Guide\n\n| \\(p\\) range | Best formula |\n|---|---|\n| Near 0 (start) | Newton–Gregory Forward |\n| Near \\(n\\) (end) | Newton–Gregory Backward |\n| Near 0, going forward | Gauss Forward |\n| Near 0, going backward | Gauss Backward |\n| \\(p \\approx 0\\), symmetric | Stirling |\n| \\(p \\approx 0.5\\) | Bessel |\n\n> **ISS tip:** Know how to compute \\(p\\), build the difference table, and substitute. The error term formula is also frequently tested."
                },
                {
                    "id": "num-divided-diff",
                    "label": "Divided Differences & Unequal Intervals",
                    "content": "## Divided Differences & Lagrange Interpolation\n\n### Divided Differences — Definition\n\n**First order:**\n\\[\nf[x_0, x_1] = \\frac{f(x_1) - f(x_0)}{x_1 - x_0}\n\\]\n\n**Second order:**\n\\[\nf[x_0, x_1, x_2] = \\frac{f[x_1,x_2] - f[x_0,x_1]}{x_2 - x_0}\n\\]\n\n**General \\(n\\)-th order:**\n\\[\nf[x_0,x_1,\\ldots,x_n] = \\frac{f[x_1,\\ldots,x_n] - f[x_0,\\ldots,x_{n-1}]}{x_n - x_0}\n\\]\n\n### Properties of Divided Differences\n\n- **Symmetric** in its arguments: \\(f[x_0,x_1] = f[x_1,x_0]\\)\n- If \\(f(x)\\) is a polynomial of degree \\(n\\), then \\(f[x_0,\\ldots,x_n]\\) = leading coefficient, and \\(f[x_0,\\ldots,x_{n+1}] = 0\\)\n- **Leibniz rule:** \\(\\Delta^n[u\\cdot v]\\) can be expanded similarly to binomial\n- Relation to forward differences (equal spacing): \\(f[x_0,x_1,\\ldots,x_n] = \\dfrac{\\Delta^n f_0}{n!\\,h^n}\\)\n\n### Newton's Divided Difference Interpolation\n\n**Use: unequal intervals** (or any spacing).\n\n\\[\nf(x) = f[x_0] + (x-x_0)f[x_0,x_1] + (x-x_0)(x-x_1)f[x_0,x_1,x_2] + \\cdots\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\Bigl[\\prod_{j=0}^{k-1}(x-x_j)\\Bigr]\\, f[x_0,\\ldots,x_k]\n\\]\n\n### Lagrange's Interpolation Formula\n\n**Use: unequal intervals; compact form.**\n\n\\[\nf(x) = \\sum_{i=0}^{n} L_i(x)\\, f(x_i)\n\\]\n\nwhere the **Lagrange basis polynomial** is:\n\\[\nL_i(x) = \\prod_{\\substack{j=0 \\\\ j\\neq i}}^{n} \\frac{x - x_j}{x_i - x_j}\n\\]\n\n**Error:**\n\\[\nR_n(x) = \\frac{f^{(n+1)}(\\xi)}{(n+1)!}\\prod_{i=0}^{n}(x-x_i), \\quad \\xi \\in (x_0, x_n)\n\\]\n\n**Note:** Lagrange does not require a difference table but is computationally heavier for large \\(n\\).\n\n### Inverse Interpolation\n\nGiven \\(y\\), find \\(x\\). Two methods:\n1. **Lagrange applied inversely** — swap roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\)\n2. **Iterative/successive approximation** using forward formula and solving for \\(p\\)\n\n> **ISS tip:** Divided difference symmetry and the relation \\(\\Delta^n f_0 = n!\\,h^n f[x_0,\\ldots,x_n]\\) are common MCQ targets. Also know that Lagrange can be derived from Newton's divided difference formula."
                },
                {
                    "id": "num-integration",
                    "label": "Numerical Integration",
                    "content": "## Numerical Integration (Quadrature)\n\nAll rules below approximate \\(\\displaystyle I = \\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx\\) with \\(n\\) sub-intervals of width \\(h = (b-a)/n\\).\n\n---\n\n### Trapezoidal Rule\n\n\\(n\\) must be ≥ 1 (any integer).\n\n\\[\nI \\approx \\frac{h}{2}\\Bigl[f_0 + 2(f_1+f_2+\\cdots+f_{n-1}) + f_n\\Bigr]\n\\]\n\n**Error:** \\(\\displaystyle -\\frac{(b-a)h^2}{12}f''(\\xi)\\) — error is \\(O(h^2)\\).\n\n---\n\n### Simpson's 1/3 Rule\n\n**Requirement:** \\(n\\) must be **even**.\n\n\\[\nI \\approx \\frac{h}{3}\\Bigl[f_0 + 4f_1 + 2f_2 + 4f_3 + 2f_4 + \\cdots + 4f_{n-1} + f_n\\Bigr]\n\\]\n\nPattern of coefficients: **1, 4, 2, 4, 2, …, 4, 1**\n\n**Error:** \\(\\displaystyle -\\frac{(b-a)h^4}{180}f^{(4)}(\\xi)\\) — error is \\(O(h^4)\\). **Exact for polynomials up to degree 3.**\n\n---\n\n### Simpson's 3/8 Rule\n\n**Requirement:** \\(n\\) must be a **multiple of 3**.\n\n\\[\nI \\approx \\frac{3h}{8}\\Bigl[f_0 + 3f_1 + 3f_2 + 2f_3 + 3f_4 + 3f_5 + 2f_6 + \\cdots + f_n\\Bigr]\n\\]\n\nPattern: **1, 3, 3, 2, 3, 3, 2, …, 3, 3, 1**\n\n**Error:** \\(O(h^4)\\). **Exact for polynomials up to degree 3.**\n\n---\n\n### Weddle's Rule\n\n**Requirement:** \\(n\\) must be a **multiple of 6**.\n\n\\[\nI \\approx \\frac{3h}{10}\\Bigl[f_0 + 5f_1 + f_2 + 6f_3 + f_4 + 5f_5 + 2f_6 + 5f_7 + \\cdots + f_n\\Bigr]\n\\]\n\nPattern over each group of 6: **1, 5, 1, 6, 1, 5, 2** (last group ends with 1).\n\n**Error:** \\(O(h^6)\\). Very accurate for smooth functions.\n\n---\n\n### Comparison Table\n\n| Rule | Intervals needed | Coeff pattern | Exact for degree | Error order |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Trapezoidal | any \\(n\\) | 1,2,2,...,2,1 | ≤1 | \\(O(h^2)\\) |\n| Simpson's 1/3 | even \\(n\\) | 1,4,2,4,...,4,1 | ≤3 | \\(O(h^4)\\) |\n| Simpson's 3/8 | mult. of 3 | 1,3,3,2,3,3,...,1 | ≤3 | \\(O(h^4)\\) |\n| Weddle's | mult. of 6 | 1,5,1,6,1,5,2,... | ≤5 | \\(O(h^6)\\) |\n\n> **ISS tip:** The most commonly tested facts are: Simpson's 1/3 requires **even** \\(n\\); its error is \\(O(h^4)\\) despite looking \\(O(h^2)\\); and the coefficient patterns. Weddle's rule is a special corrected form of the 3/8 rule."
                },
                {
                    "id": "num-summation",
                    "label": "Summation of Series",
                    "content": "## Summation of Series\n\n### Case 1: General Term is \\(\\Delta f(r)\\)\n\nIf \\(t_r = \\Delta f(r) = f(r+1) - f(r)\\), then:\n\\[\n\\sum_{r=a}^{b} t_r = f(b+1) - f(a)\n\\]\nThis is the **discrete analogue of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus**.\n\n**Example:** \\(\\sum r(r+1) = \\sum \\Delta\\!\\left(\\frac{r(r+1)(r+2)}{3}\\right)\\)\n\nUseful factorial-form antidifferences:\n\\[\n\\sum r^{(n)} = \\frac{r^{(n+1)}}{n+1} + C\n\\]\n\n### Case 2: General Term in Geometric Progression\n\nIf \\(t_r = A \\cdot x^r\\) (geometric):\n\\[\n\\sum_{r=0}^{n-1} A\\cdot x^r = A\\cdot\\frac{x^n - 1}{x - 1}, \\quad x \\neq 1\n\\]\n\nFor mixed terms like \\(t_r = P(r)\\cdot x^r\\) (poly × geometric), use the **summation by parts** technique:\n\\[\n\\sum u\\,\\Delta v = uv - \\sum Eu\\,\\Delta^{-1}(\\Delta v)\n\\]\n\n### Euler–Maclaurin Summation (reference)\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{r=a}^{b} f(r) = \\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx + \\tfrac{1}{2}[f(a)+f(b)] + \\sum_{k=1}^{p} \\frac{B_{2k}}{(2k)!}[f^{(2k-1)}(b) - f^{(2k-1)}(a)] + R_p\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(B_{2k}\\) are Bernoulli numbers: \\(B_2=1/6,\\;B_4=-1/30,\\ldots\\)\n\n> **ISS tip:** Most ISS questions on summation ask you to express the general term in factorial form, then apply the antidifference operator. Recognise \\(\\Delta(x^{(n)}) = n\\cdot x^{(n-1)}\\)."
                },
                {
                    "id": "num-ode",
                    "label": "Numerical Solution of ODEs",
                    "content": "## Numerical Solutions of Differential Equations\n\nSolving \\(dy/dx = f(x,y)\\), \\(y(x_0) = y_0\\), step size \\(h\\).\n\n---\n\n### Euler's Method (Single-step, explicit)\n\n\\[\ny_{n+1} = y_n + h\\,f(x_n, y_n)\n\\]\n\n- Simplest method; **first-order** accurate, error \\(O(h^2)\\) per step, \\(O(h)\\) globally.\n- Geometrically: follow the tangent at each step.\n- Modified Euler (Heun's): predictor–corrector, \\(O(h^2)\\) accuracy.\n\n---\n\n### Picard's Method (Successive Approximation)\n\nBased on converting ODE to integral equation:\n\\[\ny(x) = y_0 + \\int_{x_0}^{x} f\\bigl(t, y(t)\\bigr)\\,dt\n\\]\n\nIterative scheme:\n\\[\ny^{(k+1)}(x) = y_0 + \\int_{x_0}^{x} f\\bigl(t, y^{(k)}(t)\\bigr)\\,dt, \\quad y^{(0)} = y_0\n\\]\n\n- Gives solution as a **power series** (polynomial approximation).\n- Not practical for large ranges; mainly theoretical.\n\n---\n\n### Runge–Kutta 4th Order Method (RK4)\n\nMost important method for ISS — **4th-order accurate**, error \\(O(h^4)\\) per step.\n\n\\[\nk_1 = h\\,f(x_n,\\, y_n)\n\\]\n\\[\nk_2 = h\\,f\\!\\left(x_n + \\tfrac{h}{2},\\; y_n + \\tfrac{k_1}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nk_3 = h\\,f\\!\\left(x_n + \\tfrac{h}{2},\\; y_n + \\tfrac{k_2}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nk_4 = h\\,f\\!\\left(x_n + h,\\; y_n + k_3\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\tfrac{1}{6}(k_1 + 2k_2 + 2k_3 + k_4)\n\\]\n\nWeighted average: weights **1:2:2:1** summing to 6.\n\n---\n\n### Milne's Method (Multi-step, Predictor–Corrector)\n\n**Requires 4 prior values**: \\(y_{n-3}, y_{n-2}, y_{n-1}, y_n\\).\n\n**Predictor:**\n\\[\ny_{n+1}^{(P)} = y_{n-3} + \\frac{4h}{3}\\bigl[2f_n - f_{n-1} + 2f_{n-2}\\bigr]\n\\]\n\n**Corrector:**\n\\[\ny_{n+1}^{(C)} = y_{n-1} + \\frac{h}{3}\\bigl[f_{n-1} + 4f_n + f_{n+1}^{(P)}\\bigr]\n\\]\n\nThe corrector is Simpson's 1/3 rule applied to the ODE.\n\n---\n\n### Comparison\n\n| Method | Type | Starts from | Order | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Euler | Single-step | \\(y_0\\) only | 1st | Simplest; low accuracy |\n| Picard | Single-step | \\(y_0\\) only | Series | Theoretical; exact in limit |\n| RK4 | Single-step | \\(y_0\\) only | 4th | Best single-step for ISS |\n| Milne | Multi-step | 4 prior values | 4th | Needs RK4 to start |\n\n> **ISS tip:** RK4 is the most tested. Know all four \\(k\\) values and the \\(1:2:2:1\\) weights. Milne is predictor–corrector — the corrector is Simpson's rule. Picard gives a polynomial approximation by repeated integration."
                }
